---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="OpenBazaar Alternatives in 2026: What Still Works - Mobazha"
  description="OpenBazaar shut down in 2021. Here are the projects still running, what they do differently, and how to start selling with crypto today."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20">
    <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-[var(--color-text-primary)] sm:text-5xl">
        OpenBazaar is gone. Here is what replaced it.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        OpenBazaar was the most serious attempt anyone made at a decentralized marketplace. It worked, for a while. Then OB1 ran out of money, and in 2021 the company walked away.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        The code did not disappear. It was open source, and that is the reason there is anything to talk about today.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">What happened</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        OpenBazaar launched with a simple idea: no platform, no middleman, no fees taken by a company. Two people trade directly, and the software handles the rest.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        The problem was never the idea. It was everything around it. Discovery was hard, disputes were hard, and most buyers never found a reason to open the app twice.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        When OB1 shut down, the network did not die instantly. It faded. Stores went offline. The subreddit went quiet. Today it gets about twenty visits a week.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">What replaced it</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">Three kinds of things, and they are not equivalent.</p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <li><strong>Projects built on the same code.</strong> Mobazha is a fork of the OpenBazaar codebase, started by members of the original community in late 2021. The core idea is unchanged: sellers own their store, and no platform sits in the middle of the money.</li>
        <li><strong>Independent decentralized markets.</strong> Particl Marketplace and Zano BaZar take a different technical route but aim at the same problem.</li>
        <li><strong>Centralized crypto marketplaces.</strong> Paxful and NoOnes were the biggest. Both are gone now, Paxful in 2025 and NoOnes in August 2026.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">What Mobazha does differently</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        The original OpenBazaar assumed people would find each other. They did not. Mobazha adds the parts that were missing.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <strong>Escrow that does not require trust in the platform.</strong> Payments use 2-of-3 multisig. The buyer, the seller and a neutral third party each hold a key. No single party can move the money alone.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <strong>A real dispute process.</strong> If the two sides cannot agree, an independent arbitrator decides. Arbitrators set their own fees and languages and never touch shipping or the money.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <strong>Discovery and supply.</strong> Sellers can list products from suppliers who hold stock in several countries and handle storage and shipping. That removes the two hardest parts of starting: sourcing and logistics.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <strong>Portability.</strong> Store identity, listings and customer relationships belong to the seller. You can run your own node, and you can move your store to another one.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">What is still missing</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Honestly: buyers. Decentralized marketplaces have never solved demand. If you are looking for Amazon-scale traffic, this is not it yet.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        What you get instead is control. Nobody can close your store because an algorithm did not like your listing. Nobody can freeze your balance, because there is no balance to freeze.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">How to start</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Create a store, list one item, share the link. If you want to run your own node, you can. If you do not, use the hosted version and move later.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        The migration from OpenBazaar is not automatic, but it is not complicated either: your listings are text and images, and your customers are people you can tell where to find you.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-8">
        <a class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-brand-light)] underline" href="https://app.mobazha.org/">Open a store on Mobazha</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
